{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/632915-2ed-five-big-covenants-around-the-world","id":632915,"title":"[2ED] Five Big Covenants Around the World","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-19T18:34:18-07:00","id":632915,"title":"[2ED] Five Big Covenants Around the World","content":"I proper to run <b>Beast </b>official SAS from corebook and I'm doing so catching up with Requiem 2ED for the crossover puopuse, even if I am rather passerby fan of setting ( more &quot;reading like vampire works as NPCs&quot; than playing chronicles ).<br /> <br /> One thing that strike me in 2ED corebook is that Lancea et Sanctum sounds now as it is only Catholics thing - and we do not have like Protestant, Islam or Judaism version of Second Estate. For a first I could see it cannot work - LS is great Church, not working with various little congregations of Protestant faiths. But Islam and Judaism variants would not be as much different than Catholics based and I would rather suspect to have a bit different flavor LS in Middle East, but <b>still</b> Lancea et Sanctum at the heart - at least Islam is treating Jesus as a prophet, so I suspect vampire after him - the Longinus - would also work for Muslim vampires.<br /> <br /> Second is Circle of the Crone - Am I get it right they are random cults up until XIX century and then are transformed in to proper Covenant by mass disobiediance in Scotland? Something like more limited Great Refusal of Free Council in <b>Mage</b>? Because we have settings like New Orlean, where we have both Baron Cimiti\u00e8re and Nathanial Blanch factions that seems like two almost completely different miniCovenants in to themselves. How could mish-mash like that work as one Coveneat? In New Orlean voodoo vampires would be much more older than XIX pagans of &quot;Circle&quot;. What even more disturbing is that Cruac should steam from local believes - or does it all now Irish and Scotland &quot;blood magic&quot;, with previous pagan being sticked to mainstream Circle powers? Baron would rather do not want his and his ancestors powers to be called &quot;lesser magic&quot; or &quot;not proper&quot;. How Cimiti\u00e8re and Blanch work inside one group - they faiths are almost uncompatible...<br /> <br /> To end this post, we have Invictus - that is said to evolve naturally in local setting with groups connected to power. Where 2ED Lancea and Circle sounds as too strict and force to me to work on global scale, the Invictus was left to be decentralized and local colorful as much as possible. Why is that a matter of things, I ask?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T05:29:13-07:00","id":633343,"title":"","content":"The Lancea Sanctum is Christian, not just Catholic. If a Protestant, Eastern Orthodox, or Oriental Orthodox church was the Christian church dominating the locale then those would be who the Lancea Sanctum are associated with. Islam and Judaism are not covered with the Lancea Sanctum, although I think you could actually just use a variant form of the Lancea Sanctum where those religions are dominant. In that case, you should lose the tie to Christians when using the Lancea Sanctum. That does not seem to be the intention. I think the intention was for Lancea Sanctum to remain Christian and for a place where Islam or Judaism is more prevalent the Invictus would have more religious overtones, without Theban though.<br /> <br /> In New Orleans, those two mini-covenants would be factions of the Circle of the Crone, or Baron Cimitiere (IIRC wasn't a Crone) would run a mini-covenant. They would be two Circle cults in the same location that could be loosely allied, strongly allied, or enemies. The Circle isn't big on rules. The decision is based on what the Baron might use as a Houngan to work his magic and personal choice. If he uses Cruac then his group is Circle too. If he uses something else or nothing (no real magic) then he might not associate with the Circle at all. Either way, the groups would be independent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T07:56:39-07:00","id":633479,"title":"","content":"I don't think of the Covenants as big global organizations, or even global doctrines. But more like schools of thought that get passed around - what works tends to be common in many Domains, but each will have its own variations. And although there might be lines of loyalty from individual to individual between Domains, there's no global authority.<br /> <br /> I think the best analogue is really political parties. Plenty of countries have a Green party, a Conservative party, a Social Democratic party, etc. Does that mean the Green Party in Canada and the Green Party in Germany share organization, have the same policies, and are beholden to some Global Green Council? Certainly not. But they probably have a lot in common, and if you're aligned with the Greens in Germany, when you move to Canada you'll probably look first at the local Greens...though some of their priorities might seem quite strange.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T10:49:33-07:00","id":633580,"title":"","content":"Wade L has the right of it. Its why the global section of Vampire Second Edition goes into the strangeness cities around the world get up to. Making the Covenants into Tier 3 Conspiracies that actively spread a core doctrine around the world basically changes up their presentation quite a bit. Danse Macabre gives neat ideas on how that can be achieved, but don't limit yourself to just those views. Focusing on certain aspects over others will usually yield all kinds of whackiness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/717-monghani","name":"Monghani","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar717_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T14:33:44-07:00","id":633801,"title":"","content":"First of, the five Covenants are just sample. They are the most widespread Covenants, but far from the only ones out there. So, if a Covenant doesn't fit in your city, just scrap it out. If you need another group with other doctrines, just implement it.<br /> <br /> The Lancea et Sanctum IS Roman Catholic at the core. It was formed by early Catholics, took a mold in the Medieval Catholic Church, and tries to keep ties to the Catholic community in the cities where it is present. That said, though, it isn't the same institution and lacks the centralization, so distinct communities of the LeS can have a lot of variations. So you CAN have a city where the LeS commune with other christian churches and take local customs to their rites, but that can be neighboring heresy if taken too far. Usually non-catholic vampires do find the LeS strange. You can even do non-christian LeS, but that is positively heresy. Of course, a heresy that covers an entire city can be completely impossible to get rid of, so the LeS would likely ignore them.<br /> <br /> But that said, it is simple to take the LeS as a basis to create other religious Covenants, or just let the local vampires without one. You don't need a Covenant to be religious, and local Kindred can simply have common personal interpretations over their state in the view of the mortal religions they follow. Jew and Muslim Kindred, in special, I imagine simply following their religions mostly as they did before. A Jew is, still, a Jew, and maybe a Vampire Rabbi can open the synagogue for the night to the undead community. I don't even know if mosques close at night, and at least some musallas don't, so Muslim vampires have an easy time just being Muslims after death.<br /> <br /> Circle of the Crone created Cruac as a mix of traditions, so it is theoretically a corpus of rites that you can adapt to any religious magic tradition that can follow the precept of blood magic and sacrifices. Curiously, that puts some mystic Jewish traditions under Cruac. That said, it is also an alliance of pagan religions against the Lancea et Sanctum's power. Distinct communities of the Circle can have entirely distinct religions, and if some can be very open and even have various religions in one group, others can even openly fight against each other if they don't need for now to work together to defend themselves against the Lancea. Again, outside a context where the CotC and the LeS are at each one's throat, you can get rid of the CotC as a Covenant if it pleases you.<br /> <br /> Invictus isn't less centralized than CotC and LeS. Those aren't centralized either, they don't operate in global scale. None of the Covenants do. So the Invictus is globally kind of a club that spreads ideas about how to achieve and maintain power, but indeed work in practice in the local scale. But, again, that's what EVERY Covenant do. Their true global power is almost non-existent. And &quot;lesser&quot; Covenants abound, just as powerful where they are.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T16:24:49-07:00","id":633930,"title":"","content":"Looking at some of the merits in the <i>Secrets of the Covenants </i>open development blogs opens the door to speculation about a few of the Covenants. While I think it's rather ironic that the Invictus don't have a centralized hierarchical command structure, the existence of the InBoard and other ideas are very suggestive of how extensive they are. The Sanctified having a special capacity to harbor criminal refugees too, also speaks to how widespread they are. Those two are also some of the few Covenants we know of to have a continuous existence for almost 2000 years, so it's not really surprising if they can be found in a lot of places.<br /> <br /> Like the Mage Orders (if not moreso) the key is to understand that while these are organization which can be found in all four corners of the globe, they are not centralized.<br /> <br /> This does not mean that they don't talk or form alliances over distance, though. In fact, the whole concept of the 'cloister' in 2e's core neatly puts the idea to rest that they <i>don't </i>talk to one another. Rather, maintaining a powerbase over multiple domains can be very difficult, and while the Invictus in New York and Philadelphia might talk and send people between them, that probably doesn't translate into a single chain of command in both cities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T16:41:56-07:00","id":633964,"title":"","content":"The impression I get is that the Covenants more-or-less went global as a result of European imperialism. Where Christian missionaries went, so too did the Lancea et Sanctum (which, properly speaking, predates the Catholic Church and probably has members who, in life, adhered to pretty much all forms of Christianity). Where the nations of Europe imposed their own power structures on foreign lands, so too did the Invictus expand their conspiracy. Where European science and mysticism captured the imagination of local vampires, there did the Ordo Dracul find new recruits. The Circle of the Crone and the Carthian movement emerged, to a large extent, in response to the persecution of the Lancea et Sanctum and the excesses of the Invictus, and they're pretty much found in the same places that the older covenants are.<br /> <br /> That said, it's also true that the Covenants aren't monolithic entities. They're more like philosophies, ways of being undead, and as a result, similar bodies have arisen from time to time all over the world, though they would certainly use different names.<br /> <br /> Anyway, like the European colonial powers, the Covenants met resistance in many places where they tried to impose their own vision of the Requiem. Even in a place like Athens, close to the heartland of the European covenants, local traditions often trump more widespread groups. While the Cthonic cults of Greece have mostly thrown in with the Circle of the Crone, the Kataram\u00e9non practices a weird blend of pagan Apollonian traditions with Greek Orthodox Christianity. Due to the influence of this old and powerful faith, the Lancea et Sanctum haven't been able to make inroads in the city, despite the fact that the Greek-speaking Byzantine empire was once one of the most vibrant centers of their faith.<br /> <br /> Go to some place like Beijing, and you leave the familiar covenants almost entirely behind. The mystical schools known as the Way of the Dragon and the Dragon's Path <i>resemble</i> the Ordo Dracul in their goals, and have been influenced by <i>Rites of the Dragon</i> in modern times, but their methods are steeped in Chinese philosophy. Meanwhile, temporal power is held in the city by a bureaucracy of the Damned made up of ministries that can be as fractious as the Covenants in western cities. The Lancea et Sanctum is there, as might be expected, but are of little significance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T04:54:18-07:00","id":634459,"title":"","content":"I mostly agree with <b>CatDoom</b> on the evolution of Covenants. I only question how &quot;European Kindred imperialism&quot; would affect Islam vampires or how Circle of Crone would incorporate the voodoonists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T06:31:33-07:00","id":634530,"title":"","content":"I have no problem with jews being part of the Lancea et Sanctum. Accoding Requiem for Rome, it was originally a jewish faction (the Sanctum) of the Peregrina College. In first century of christian era, they open their membership to other marginal faiths of the Empire, and one of them were the Longinians (the Lancea). With time, the Longinian doctrine became dominant and eventually it was the one which defined orthodoxy in the covenant. So, a jewish faction is perfectly ok as a remnant of its forgotten past.<br /> <br /> But a muslim faction, I don't see it. I prefer them as an independent covenant, the Banu Shaitan, as they were presented in Ancient Mysteries. They even could be an split faction of the Sanctified, but having becoming an independent covenant (with the rise of Islam, a lot of Sanctified could have join them), but I doubt they identify themselves as being from the same group than the Sanctified. But, even with european imperialism, I cannot see them merging with Sanctified in the XIXth century. Being similar, haing influences, yes, of course. But merging?<br /> <br /> The poblem with first edition was that everything had to be included in the big five. Although the corebook spoke about other covenants, that was an aspect almost neglected (with some exceptions). I would have loved covenant books similar to the bloodline books. But now, with second edition, the core setting showed us how diverse could be vampire society and covenants much better than all first edition books together. I think it's a good moment to reboot the Banu Shaitan as a full independnet covenant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>LAND OF THE DAMNED: SPAIN (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/3MHWYm\" target=\"_blank\">Land of the Damned: Spain</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/52b0TX\" target=\"_blank\">Kingdoms of Blood: Spain</a>; Cities of the Damned: <a href=\"https://goo.gl/XFMSSe\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/AXxdgq\" target=\"_blank\">Valencia</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/zkI5a1\" target=\"_blank\">Carthian Constitution</a> (1812), <a href=\"https://goo.gl/5H8M4s\" target=\"_blank\">Three Arrows Pact</a>: <br /> <br /> <b>OTHERS (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/36HngB\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: The Redemption</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/6B9V8l\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Forgotten</a>","edited":{"userid":247,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/247-uxas","user":"Uxas","date":". 08-21-2015, 09:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/247-uxas","name":"Uxas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T07:15:01-07:00","id":634573,"title":"","content":"Having separate covenants makes more sense for the Lancea et Sanctum, especially since the Theban sorceries were found and not necessarily something everyone would have access to. Muslim covenants wouldn't be as well and probably not as strong outside of Africa and the Middle East.<br /> <br /> I don't really understand what wyrdhamsters getting at with the Circle of the Covenant.<br /> <br /> Secrets of the Covenants is coming out eventually, and will probably give you a better perspective than the three pages per covenant you're basing thoughts on second edition on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 08-21-2015, 10:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T09:34:36-07:00","id":634712,"title":"","content":"Though the Lancea et Sanctum started with Jews and a variety of Christians, I don't think its modern incarnation takes well (usually) with religions too dissimilar with the Catholic (though I wouldn't distinguish too much Roman and Orthodox in them). Precisely because all that came from the Covenant having a history too linked with the very formation of the Catholic Church. Sure, there where Jews in the start. Christianity originated as a Jewish heresy, after all. But as time passed, power, politic and prejudice resulted in the Jews being ostracized by a Church that persecuted heresy with a vengeance rarely seen before. It makes all sense that the Lancea et Sanctum performed its own purges, for similar reasons, specially keeping in mind that new members where coming from this same conflict context. A Christian that grew up hating Jews would have little to no reason at all to set this hatred aside after the Embrace.<br /> <br /> Of course it is hypocrisy, given that the Lancea itself is just another Christian heresy of the time, but they mimicked and followed the history of Catholicism, and given time where formed primarily by Catholics-in-Life. It is the same reason that most of the themes and MOs of the Covenant are based on the Catholic Church. It doesn't mean they don't have a greater variety than Catholicism itself, specially since the Reformation. They lack centralization and the power to deal with greater heresies. I can picture some heresies like the Bogomils faring in the Lancea far better than among the living, at least until overcrowded by new members that didn't lived with the heresy and demanded a more orthodox Church of the Damned. But still, the Lancea defines itself by relationship with the living Church. Even because I can't picture too many cities where the majority of vampire population is over 100 years. In most, even in Europe, more than half will be less than 50 years.<br /> <br /> It also goes the other way around. When a Protestant comes to undeath and finds the Lancea, what she finds is a church completely alien for her. Maybe she'll convert given her new context. Most of the times she'll just keep her faith to herself and (un)live in another Covenant or none at all. But it is unlikely that she'll still consider herself in the same church when (and if) attending mass. If a community really finds need for a church more in concert with their beliefs, it is more probable to develop a new Covenant than trying to be accepted at a church they don't even believe in. But they can also simply go to their mortal churches early in the night.<br /> <br /> A medieval Covenant of Jews is likely to exist. Previously weak and scattered, trying to support Jewish communities over Europe, now they have a stronghold in Israel. But a Muslim Covenant is unlikely to be old. For most of the history of Islam it was both predominant where found but decentralized. Islam have a precept of individual interpretation of the faith and the Koran, so it never had an organization in the mold of Catholic Church or any other church for most of it. For a Muslim vampire it would be simpler to just adapt. Probably they had groups to discuss faith as their living counterparts had, but not the need for a Covenant. Their need for it could start at the Crusades, first as a military Covenant, and them it would take another turn and a completely new shape in the second half of the 20th century, the turning point for the Islamic world. Even them, a lot of Muslim neonates can be shocked at how different the Elders and even some Ancilla are about the faith.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T10:38:26-07:00","id":634838,"title":"","content":"Well, this is my personal take on it. <br /> <br /> Each of the seven covenants (including VII and Brood) have a covenant &quot;power&quot; - Oaths and Law, holy sorcery and blood magic, Coils and Investments, and VII's blessings. These seven abilities are universal throughout the world. Learning and adapting to each set of powers brings its own restrictions and mindset along with it. <br /> <br /> Each local iteration of those mindsets form the basis of a covenant. The Lancea et Sanctum is, at heart, primarily Catholic. There are Jewish, Islamic, and Egyptian covenants that all use Theban, plus likely some Hindu as well as others, throughout the world. The non-Lance have different histories and beliefs, but they all practice Theban Sorcery (possibly by another name), develop a focus on academia as a result, and (due to vampiric nature) use said academia to revision history, and so on. <br /> <br /> The Circle of the Crone is basically vampire-neopaganism, with a heavy dose of corrupt Wicca. Voodoo do not fit into the Circle of the Crone as it is. The two have different histories, cultures, beliefs. They share a common mindset, however, and practice of blood magic. <br /> <br /> In Tibet, there's a Buddhist-themed covenant run by the Naga Lord, and learn &quot;treasures&quot; that work exactly like Coils, but it is not the same scientific cult that worships Dracula. <br /> <br /> The same holds true for any vampire nobles or manipulative underdog rebels. Each has different cultures and histories from the Invictus and Carthians, but spiritually they act a lot a like. Even in Tokyo, where we have three vampire covenants, they act suspiciously like a local variation of the Invictus, the Carthians, and a shinto-inspired blood witch.<br /> <br /> For the fun of it, I state that these universal principles aren't just tropes or convergent evolution, but actual forces in the vampire world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T12:03:44-07:00","id":634923,"title":"","content":"Muslim imperialism has existed about as long as the Invictus. I don't know that there really would be any mechanical differences. I expect the Invictus by another name would be the dominant covenant in &quot;Muslim countries.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Circle is about returning to the &quot;old ways&quot; and similar such that Voodoo would fit in nicely. It would be different than the roots of the Circle so it would be another version, but basically and mechanically the same thing.<br /> <br /> A Voodoo Circle would be the Circle of the Crone only with Voodoo. A Muslim Invictus would be like the Invictus only with Muslims. Names, histories, beliefs, and cultures would vary as necessary to respect Voodoo or Islam.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T11:38:25-07:00","id":636110,"title":"","content":"I particularly like the idea of those seven principles being just examples or convergent evolution in most of my games, actually, but the notion of them as actual forces in the vampire world isn't bad for some specific chronicles. Of course, the convergent evolution idea is mostly a means to an end: simplify things using the same rules again and again, so you don't have to reinvent the wheel each time you do some little variation of something (like they did to exhaustion in Masquerade).<br /> <br /> Over Muslim countries I would expect variations of Invictus and Ordo Dracul composed only of faithful, but nothing like the Lancea et Sanctum or the Circle of the Crone based on Islam proper. Of course, those can still be present as foreign religions. Or maybe scholar Covenants similar to the Ordo but working with either form of ritualistic sorcery instead of Coils.<br /> <br /> The Voodoo Circle, as any other of the various religions born of African roots mingled with Christianity, could still be found in the Circle for political reasons. The Circle IS already an ecumenical group by definition, formed to fight against the oppression of a Christian majority, and linked by a system of blood magic created to empower them against their common enemies. I can easily see the Circle proper, or something very similar, working wherever something akin to paganism exists as a minority group. It is formed and characterized by religion, but is ultimately a political group with view on self-defense.<br /> <br /> Curiously, that means that some places could have another kind of change: a Circle of the Crone proper, but without Cr\u00faac, because it doesn't works well with the religions involved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}